.TH TARPM "1" "September 2026" "tarpm"
.SH NAME
tarpm \- list, create, modify, and extract RPM packages without using
rpmbuild(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] [\f[B]\-?\f[R]] [\f[B]\-V\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] \f[B]\-t\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R]
\f[I]RPMFILENAME\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] \f[B]\-x\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R]
\f[I]RPMFILENAME\f[R] [\f[B]\-O\f[R] \f[I]DIRNAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-L\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]] [\f[B]\-S\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-H\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]] [\f[B]\-P\f[R] \f[I]DIRNAME\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] \f[B]\-c\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R]
\f[I]RPMFILENAME\f[R]
[\f[B]\-H\f[R] \f[I]PATH\f[R]] [\f[B]\-P\f[R] \f[I]DIRNAME\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-F\f[R] \f[I]NUMBER\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-m\f[R]] [\f[B]\-u\f[R]] [\f[B]\-g\f[R]] [\f[B]\-l\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-a\f[R]] \f[I]DIRECTORY\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] is a command line tool for working with RPM package
files. The interface is intended to mimic the command line program
tar(1), but with necessary options specific to RPM files. When you
extract an RPM file, a subdirectory is created matching the NEVRA of
the package, unless you name a directory with \f[B]\-O\f[R]. Inside
this directory, you will find a subdirectory called
\f[B]payload\f[R] which contains all of the files and directories in
the package.  At the same level of the \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory
you will find JSON files containing the RPM metadata.  These files may
be edited and the contents of the \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory may
be altered before creating a new RPM package.
.PP
Keep in mind the source RPM and corresponding spec file are not used
by \f[B]tarpm\f[R].  You are working with individual packages
only. Care must be taken when modifying the metadata or the payload
contents as rpm(8) may refuse to work with your newly created package.
.PP
Some of the JSON metadata is for informational purposes only and may
not be modified. See below for more details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-?\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Display command usage information.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Display version information.
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R], \f[B]\-\-list\f[R]
List the contents of the RPM package without metadata (cannot be used
with \f[B]\-c\f[R] or \f[B]\-x\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-x\f[R], \f[B]\-\-extract\f[R]
Extract the named RPM package on the command line (cannot be used with
\f[B]\-t\f[R] or \f[B]\-c\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R], \f[B]\-\-create\f[R]
Create the named RPM package on the command line from the named
DIRECTORY contents (cannot be used with \f[B]\-t\f[R] or
\f[B]\-x\f[R]).  The DIRECTORY must contain RPM package metadata in
JSON format, like what you see when you extract an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Verbose progress output.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R] FILENAME, \f[B]\-\-filename\f[R]=FILENAME
The name of the input or output RPM file.
.TP
\f[B]\-O\f[R] DIRNAME, \f[B]\-\-output\f[R]=DIRNAME
The name of the output directory when extracting an RPM.  Without this
option the extracted package goes in to a subdirectory of the
directory you are in named after the NEVRA of the package.
.TP
\f[B]\-L\f[R] PATH, \f[B]\-\-lead\f[R]=PATH
Where \f[B]lead.json\f[R] is written when extracting an RPM.  A
relative PATH is taken from the directory you are in.  A PATH ending
in a slash or naming a directory gets \f[B]lead.json\f[R] added to
it.  Any directory in PATH that is not there yet is created.  A PATH
of a single hyphen writes the JSON to standard output instead of to a
file.  The RPM lead is rebuilt from the header, so this is not read
when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-S\f[R] PATH, \f[B]\-\-signature\f[R]=PATH
Where \f[B]signature.json\f[R] is written when extracting an RPM.
PATH works the same way it does for \f[B]\-\-lead\f[R].  A PATH of a
single hyphen may only be used when extracting an RPM.  The signature
header is generated, so this is not read when creating an RPM and
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error if you give it anyway.
.TP
\f[B]\-H\f[R] PATH, \f[B]\-\-header\f[R]=PATH
Where \f[B]header.json\f[R] is written when extracting an RPM and
where it is read from when creating one.  PATH works the same way it
does for \f[B]\-\-lead\f[R].  A PATH of a single hyphen may only be
used when extracting an RPM.  Tag values that go to their own file,
such as \f[B]description.txt\f[R], sit in the same directory as
\f[B]header.json\f[R], or in the output directory when the header goes
to standard output.
.TP
\f[B]\-P\f[R] DIRNAME, \f[B]\-\-payload\f[R]=DIRNAME
Where the payload tree is written when extracting an RPM and where it
is read from when creating one.  A relative DIRNAME is taken from the
directory you are in.  When extracting, DIRNAME is created if it is
not there yet and no \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory is made in the
output directory.  When creating, DIRNAME has to be a directory that
is already there.
.TP
\f[B]\-F\f[R] NUMBER, \f[B]\-\-format\f[R]=NUMBER
The RPM format to write the package as, which is either 4 or 6.  The
default is 4.  May only be used when creating an RPM.  This is the
same thing rpm(8) does with its %_rpmformat macro.
.RS
.PP
A v4 signature header carries the Sha1, Sha256, Size, Md5, and
Payloadsize tags along with the reserved space rpmsign(1) writes in
to.  A v6 one carries the Sha256 and Sha3_256 digests of the main
header and the reserved space, and nothing else.
.PP
The main header carries the payload digests the format calls for.  A
v4 package gets Payloadsha256, Payloadsha256alt, and the
Payloaddigestalgo tag naming the algorithm.  A v6 package gets the
Rpmformat tag, the Payloadsize and Payloadsizealt tags, and the
Payloadsha256, Payloadsha512, and Payloadsha3_256 digests along with
the alt form of each.  The alt digests cover the payload with the
compression taken back off, the others cover the payload as it lands
in the package.  The tags the other format owns are taken out, so
writing a package you extracted from one format as the other leaves
nothing behind.
.PP
Each format has main header tags the other one never uses.  tarpm
takes out the ones that do not belong, so you do not have to edit
\f[B]header.json\f[R] yourself before switching formats.  The v4 tags
are Payloaddigestalgo, Fileclass, Classdict, Size, Filesizes,
Archivesize, Filesignatures, and Filesignaturelength.  The v6 tags
are Rpmformat, Payloadsize, Payloadsizealt, Filemimeindex, Mimedict,
Sourcenevr, Payloadsha512, Payloadsha512alt, Payloadsha3_256, and
Payloadsha3_256alt.
.PP
File sizes survive the switch.  A v4 package counts them in Size and
Filesizes and a v6 package counts them in Longsize and
Longfilesizes.  tarpm writes whichever pair the format wants and the
numbers stay the same.  A v4 package uses the 64 bit tags when a size
is too large for the 32 bit ones, the same as rpm(8).
.PP
Two things do not survive.  A v4 package describes each file with a
Fileclass and a v6 package describes it with a MIME type, and neither
one can be turned into the other, so the new package goes without.
The rpmlib() entries in \f[B]dependencies\f[R] are left alone as
well, so a package you convert to v6 keeps the ones rpm(8) only adds
for v4.  Edit \f[B]dependencies\f[R] yourself if you do not want
them.
.PP
The lead records the format in its major version, which is 3 for v4
and 4 for v6.  A v6 package is compressed with zstd at level 19, which
is what rpm(8) picks from v6 on.  A package whose metadata already
names zstd keeps the level it came with.  Nothing else about the
payload changes.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-mtime\f[R]
Take the timestamp of each file from the file in the payload tree
rather than from the \f[B]mtime\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].
May only be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-user\f[R]
Take the owner of each file from the file in the payload tree rather
than from the \f[B]user\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].  May only
be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-g\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-group\f[R]
Take the group of each file from the file in the payload tree rather
than from the \f[B]group\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].  May only
be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-linkto\f[R]
Take the target of each symlink from the symlink in the payload tree
rather than from the \f[B]linkto\f[R] key in \f[B]header.json\f[R].
The \f[B]size\f[R] of a symlink follows its target, so that is taken
from the payload tree too.  May only be used when creating an RPM.
.TP
\f[B]\-a\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-all\f[R]
The same as giving \f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-g\f[R], and
\f[B]\-l\f[R] together.  May only be used when creating an RPM.
.PP
The \f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-u\f[R], \f[B]\-g\f[R], \f[B]\-l\f[R], and
\f[B]\-a\f[R] options may be given in any combination, so you can take
the owner off the payload tree without taking the group off it.  Each
one only speaks for the value it names.  An entry with no file in the
payload tree, such as a %ghost, is left as \f[B]header.json\f[R] has
it.  \f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error each time it takes a
value off a file, but only when the file and \f[B]header.json\f[R] do
not already agree.
.PP
Similar to tar(1), you may run options together, such as
\f[B]\-xvf\f[R] or \f[B]\-cvf\f[R]. Likewise, the leading hyphen on
combined options like this is optional (in order to make
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] more syntax compatible with tar(1)).
.SH METADATA
RPM metadata is extracted by \f[B]tarpm\f[R] and written to JSON
files.  When you create a package with \f[B]tarpm\f[R], only
\f[B]header.json\f[R] must be present in the DIRECTORY serving as the
source for the package.  \f[B]lead.json\f[R] and
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] are optional because we never read them.
Fields that are read\-only are marked with a read\-only string set to
true. The JSON files are:
.TP
\f[B]lead.json\f[R]
The RPM lead.
Obsolete, only shown for informational purposes. \f[B]tarpm\f[R]
recreates the lead when it creates a package.  The RPM lead is the
first 96 bytes of any RPM file.
.RS
.PP
Because we build the lead from the Name, Epoch, Version, Release, and
Sourcepackage tags in \f[B]header.json\f[R], the DIRECTORY you create
a package from does not need a \f[B]lead.json\f[R] at all.  If one is
there we leave it alone, so editing it changes nothing in the package
we write.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]signature.json\f[R]
The RPM signature.
Marked with a magic number followed by the number of records and the
size of the data area. Information is stored in key=value manner but
value may be an array. The contents of the RPM signature is read\-only
as \f[B]tarpm\f[R] will recalculate all of the values when creating an
RPM.
.RS
.PP
The tags in \f[B]signature.json\f[R] are presented for informational
purposes only.  When you create an RPM with \f[B]tarpm\f[R], we build
the signature header from scratch for the format you ask for with
\f[B]\-F\f[R], so the DIRECTORY does not need a
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] at all and we do not read one that is there.
Just like the lead.  To reiterate this point, the tags in
\f[B]signature.json\f[R] carry a read\-only marker, the same as in the
\f[B]lead.json\f[R] file.
.PP
A package built by rpmbuild(1) and then signed by rpmsign(1) carries
signature tags that only rpmsign(1) can make, which are Dsa, Rsa,
Pgp, Gpg, Pgp5, Openpgp, Filesignatures, Filesignaturelength,
Veritysignatures, and Veritysignaturealgo.  \f[B]tarpm\f[R] reads all
of them out of a package and writes them to \f[B]signature.json\f[R]
so you can see what the package carries, but it leaves every one of
them out of a package it creates because making them takes the
private signing key.  Run rpmsign(1) on the new package to sign it
again.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]header.json\f[R]
The RPM header.
Contains the metadata that most users are expecting to find. Things
from the spec file, changelog information, dependency information, and
so on. It is also marked with a magic number followed by the number of
records and the size of the data area. Information is stored in
key=value manner but value may be an array.
.PP
The contents of \f[B]lead.json\f[R] and \f[B]signature.json\f[R] files
cannot be modified, but might be interesting to look at or use for
verification purposes.
.PP
The contents of the \f[B]header.json\f[R] file can be modified.
.SH HEADER.JSON
The \f[B]header.json\f[R] file is the main metadata file that can be
mostly modified. Fields that must remain untouched are magic,
reserved, and the Headerimmutable tags. The JSON file is broken up in
to arrays for easier editing via programming languages or automation
tools. The objective with the JSON structure is to avoid needing to
parse information like you may have to do reading filenames or spec
files.
.PP
The arrays in \f[B]header.json\f[R] containing metadata:
.TP
\f[B]tags\f[R]
Information seen in spec files such as Name, Version, and Release. You
will also find things automatically added by rpmbuild if the package
was generated with rpmbuild.  The tags array is a series of key=value
dicts. Each array entry contains a dict and each dict contains tag,
type, and value or file. The tag corresponds to an RPMTAG_ value from
/usr/include/rpm/rpmtag.h. The value is the actual information. The
type corresponds to the types defined in /usr/include/rpm/rpmtag.h. If
the type is an array, the value will be an array of that type.
.PP
If an entry in the tags array contains a file key rather than value,
then that key corresponds to a text file in the extracted RPM
directory. Some tags are written to text files for easier editing. The
Description, all scriptlets, and the Spec (for source RPMs only) are
all written to text files rather than placed directly in the JSON
file.
.TP
\f[B]dependencies\f[R]
RPM dependency information is written to the \f[B]dependencies\f[R]
dict. There is one dict entry (key) for each type of dependency which
is any of provides, requires, conflicts, obsoletes, recommends,
suggests, supplements, and enhances. The value for each key is an
array of dicts where each array entry is a dict consisting of at least
name and optionally comparison, version, and sense_flags. The
structure looks like this:
 :
\(lqdependencies\(rq: {
.IP
.EX
 \(dqDEP_TYPE\(dq: [
.EE
.IP
.EX
     {
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqname\(dq: \(dqVALUE\(dq,
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqcomparison\(dq: \(dqVALUE\(dq,
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqversion\(dq: \(dqVALUE\(dq,
.EE
.IP
.EX
         \(dqsense_flags\(dq: [
.EE
.IP
.EX
             \(dqFLAG\(dq, ...
.EE
.IP
.EX
         ]
.EE
.IP
.EX
     }, ...
.EE
} :
.PP
All dependencies must have at least a name. This can be given
explicitly in a spec file and correspond to a file path or a package
name. Or it can be autogenerated by rpmbuild. A dependency may also
have a comparison which can be =, <, >, <=, or >=. If it has a
comparison operator it also needs a version string. The sense_flags
are dependency flags found in the header that do not strictly map to a
comparison operator. \f[B]tarpm\f[R] converts these to string
representation and places them in the sense_flags array. You can find
a list of these in /usr/include/rpm/rpmds.h as the RPMSENSE_
macros. Most of these are autogenerated by rpmbuild.
.PP
You may change these dependency values in the JSON metadata, but keep
in mind that \f[B]tarpm\f[R] does no validation or checking other
than to make sure the metadata types are correct. It will put whatever
you tell it to put in the header. If you wish to manually create an
RPM, consider using the /usr/lib/rpm/find\-requires and
/usr/lib/rpm/find\-provides tools to generate this information for the
software you are packaging. That will align with what an RPM database
is expecting.
.TP
\f[B]files\f[R]
The file list of the package. Each array entry is a dict describing
one file, directory, symlink, or device node the package owns. RPM
spreads this information over a couple of dozen parallel arrays in the
header and stores most of it as numbers and bitfields.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] gathers all of that in to one dict per file and turns
the numbers in to names, so you edit one entry rather than count your
way through several arrays. A key is left off entirely when the file
has nothing to say for it, so most entries carry only some of these:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R]
The absolute path the file has once the package is installed. This is
the only key every entry carries. It is also where the file sits
inside the \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory.
.TP
\f[B]size\f[R]
The number of bytes in a regular file, or the length of the target of
a symlink. Other kinds of file do not carry this.
.TP
\f[B]mode\f[R]
The permission bits as a four digit octal string, such as
\(dq0755\(dq. The file type bits are not in here, they are in
\f[B]type\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
What kind of thing the entry is. One of file, dir, symlink, chardev,
blockdev, pipe, or socket.
.TP
\f[B]mtime\f[R]
The modification time as an ISO 8601 timestamp in UTC, such as
\(dq2026\-07\-16T00:00:00Z\(dq. RPM stores this as a time_t.
.TP
\f[B]user\f[R], \f[B]group\f[R]
The owner and group names. RPM records names here rather than numbers,
so the same package works on systems that number their users
differently.
.TP
\f[B]rdev\f[R]
The device number, for character and block device nodes only.
.TP
\f[B]device\f[R]
The device the file came off when the package was built. This is
almost always 1.
.TP
\f[B]digest\f[R]
The digest of the contents of a regular file as a hex string. The
algorithm is the one the Filedigestalgo tag names, which rpm defaults
to md5. Entries with no contents to digest do not carry this.
.TP
\f[B]linkto\f[R]
The target of a symlink.
.TP
\f[B]inode\f[R]
A number RPM gives out itself starting at 1. Files that share a number
are hard links of one another. These are not real inode numbers.
.TP
\f[B]class\f[R]
What kind of thing the file holds, in the style of file(1), such as
\(dqASCII text\(dq or a long ELF description. RPM only records a class
for files it recognizes, so plenty of entries do not carry this. See
FILE CLASSES below.
.TP
\f[B]langs\f[R]
An array of the languages of the file, such as the language of a
translated man page. RPM stores these as one string with bars between
the languages and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] splits it in to an array.
.TP
\f[B]colors\f[R]
An array naming the ELF class of the file, either Elf32 or Elf64. RPM
uses this to work out which of two packages wins when a multilib
install puts a 32 bit and a 64 bit copy of the same path on the
system.
.TP
\f[B]flags\f[R]
An array of the markings the file got in the spec file, such as
config, doc, ghost, license, readme, missingok, noreplace, or
artifact. RPM stores these as a bitfield.
.TP
\f[B]verifyflags\f[R]
An array naming what rpm(8) checks when verifying the file, such as
filedigest, filesize, mtime, mode, user, group, linkto, rdev, and
caps. RPM stores these as a bitfield too.
.TP
\f[B]provides\f[R]
An array of the dependencies this one file generated. Each entry looks
like a dependency from the \f[B]dependencies\f[R] dict with a type key
added saying which kind it is. Despite the name, requires show up here
as well as provides, because rpm records both against the file the
generators read them out of. The order matters and \f[B]tarpm\f[R]
keeps it, since rpm points at a run of these per file.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]changelog\f[R]
The changelog information from the spec file is stored as an array in
the \f[B]header.json\f[R] file. It is an array of dicts and each dict
corresponds to one changelog block from the spec file. Each dict
contains a timestamp key, a name key, and a text key. The timestamp
value is the date you would see on the changelog line, such as \(lqTue
Jul 21 2026\(rq. Internally these are stored as time_t values in the
header, but tarpm converts them to the human readable format. The name
value is a string containing everything after the date on the
changelog line. Usually this is the packager\(cqs name, email address,
a hyphen, and the version\-release value of that update (but without
the dist tag), but this is just a common convention. The text value is
an array of strings containing everything for that changelog
entry. The actual value in the header is one long string, but tarpm
splits that on newlines and presents it as an array in the JSON file
for easier editing. The entire array is joined together when
constructing an RPM.
.SH PAYLOAD
The \f[B]payload\f[R] subdirectory contains the RPM payload
contents. You may modify this subdirectory by adding files, removing
files, and changing what is in them. \f[B]tarpm\f[R] will update the
file metadata in the header based on the contents of this
subdirectory, so it is not necessary to manually update the
\f[B]files\f[R] array in \f[B]header.json\f[R].
.PP
When you create a package, \f[B]tarpm\f[R] walks the payload tree and
the file list together and reconciles them:
.TP
Files you removed
An entry naming a file that is no longer in the payload tree is left
out of the new package. A %ghost file is never in the payload to begin
with, so those are always kept.
.TP
Files you added
A file in the payload tree that the file list does not name gets an
entry of its own, filled in from the file itself. The directories
leading to a file the list already names do not get entries, since the
payload tree needs those to hold the files under them but the package
never owned them.
.TP
Files you changed
The type, the size, the symlink target, the device number, the digest,
and the class are taken from the file on disk rather than from the
entry. Swapping a file for a symlink, or for any other kind of thing,
rewrites the rest of the entry to suit the new type.
.TP
Keys you left out
An entry that is missing a key the header needs gets one, read off the
file in the payload tree. The owner, the group, and the device are not
things a file can answer, so those fall back to root, root, and 1.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] says so on standard error each time it fills one in.
.PP
The rest of an entry comes from the JSON and is written to the header
as you wrote it. That includes the permission bits, the mtime, the
owner, the group, the flags, the verify flags, the languages, and the
colors. Changing the mode or the timestamp of a file in the payload
tree does \f[B]not\f[R] change the package, because the entry already
says what those should be. Edit the \f[B]mode\f[R] and
\f[B]mtime\f[R] keys in \f[B]header.json\f[R] instead. A file you add
to the payload tree is the exception, since it has no entry yet, so
everything about it including its mode and its timestamp is read off
the file.
.PP
The \f[B]\-\-payload\-mtime\f[R], \f[B]\-\-payload\-user\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-payload\-group\f[R], and \f[B]\-\-payload\-linkto\f[R]
options turn that around for the value each one names. With
\f[B]\-\-payload\-mtime\f[R], for instance, the timestamp of a file in
the payload tree is what lands in the header and the \f[B]mtime\f[R]
key is disregarded. See \f[B]OPTIONS\f[R] above.
.SH FILE CLASSES
RPM records what kind of thing each file holds, in the style of
file(1), and \f[B]tarpm\f[R] shows it as the \f[B]class\f[R] key on a
file list entry. Internally this is a table of the class strings in
the package and one index per file in to that table.
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] builds both of those for you.
.PP
The class is worked out the same way rpmbuild(1) works it out. A file
that is not a regular file or a symlink gets a fixed name from its
type. A handful of path endings, such as .h and .png, get an answer
without looking at the file at all. Anything under /dev/ gets no class
because those stand in for device nodes the system makes. Everything
else goes to libmagic, the library behind file(1).
.PP
Whatever comes back is only kept if it holds one of the strings rpm
looks for, so a great many files end up with no class at all and no
\f[B]class\f[R] key. That is normal and does not mean anything is
wrong.
.PP
\f[B]tarpm\f[R] only works the class out again when the file may have
changed, which is when the file is new in the payload tree, when its
type changed, or when its contents no longer match the digest the
entry came in with. A file you did not touch keeps the class the
original package gave it.
.PP
Note that rpm(8) invents a class at query time when the one in the
package is empty, making one up from the file mode or, for a symlink,
from the link target. An empty class is therefore not always what
\(dqrpm \-qp \-\-queryformat\(dq shows you.
